/* Modern theme's option (settings) window content -- frame/header/positioning come from
   window_shell.rcss; this file only lays out the rows spliced into its #content slot.

   #panel is a genuine real-dp size, NOT native COptionWindow's own reference-resolution rect
   (190x419) -- this template assumes no resolution-based rescaling (layout-and-scaling.md's
   "there isn't a reference resolution"), so copying native's reference-pixel constants here
   produced a wildly oversized panel once combined with window_shell's own chrome (found the hard
   way, see window_shell.rml's own comment on the reverted root_scale attempt). Unlike the legacy
   theme's sprite-art chrome, this theme's frame is plain CSS gradients/borders with no fixed
   source-art width to match, so it doesn't need to match the legacy #panel's own width (230dp,
   locked to its sprite chrome's real size) -- 320dp here (widened from an earlier 300dp alongside
   the font-size bump below, so the wider label column doesn't have to shrink the control column to
   compensate) so the control column below can start nearer the row's own center, not just "wide
   enough"; window_shell's own existing 14dp/side content margins are already generous, no override
   needed the way legacy's 32dp/side shared margin needed one. Positioning is pure CSS
   (.center-both) now that dragging is off, so this file alone owns the panel's size -- nothing
   else mirrors it.

   Height (288dp) mirrors legacy's own sizing math for the same reason: sized for the tallest tab
   (Graphics, 6 checkbox rows: 6*24dp=144dp, at the 18dp row height below + 6dp margin-bottom) plus
   the tab bar (~36dp) and the always-visible close button block (~38dp), inside window_shell.rcss's
   own titled-panel insets here (10dp top + padding-top 40dp + 16dp bottom = 66dp):
   144+36+38+66=284dp minimum, +4dp buffer. Down from the original flat single-column layout's
   380dp now that content splits across tabs. */
#panel {
	width: 320dp;
	height: 288dp;
}

/* One row of tab buttons, new for this window -- not reusing my_quest_info.rcss's own
   .tab-row/.tab-btn, which are fixed reference-px position:absolute with legacy-specific sprite
   decorators, not portable to this flex/dp layout. Same "forged-metal" gradient/bevel recipe as
   base.rcss's own .btn/.btn:hover (literal copy of their decorator/border/box-shadow stack, minus
   .btn's own position:absolute/fixed width/height so these can flex-size instead) -- consistent
   with every other button in this theme rather than a bespoke flat-color look, and reads clearly
   as "a row of buttons" against this theme's dark content well (found live: plain flat colors were
   easy to mistake for "not rendering at all" / hidden behind the header). .active-tab swaps in a
   warm gold-metal shade (same bevel technique, .btn-ok's inverted-light-source-free approach, just
   gold instead of crimson) so the selected tab reads as a distinct, different metal, not just a
   recolored idle button. A small margin-top adds defensive clearance from window_shell's own
   header rail (which visually extends a little past its own box via box-shadow) on top of
   window_shell.rcss's own padding-top:40dp.

   height/line-height explicit on both the row and the button, not left to intrinsic content
   sizing via padding alone -- this engine has no box-sizing (border/padding add to a box's size,
   they don't come out of it, see .gmd-btn.cols-2's own comment), and a flex row with no explicit
   height left the buttons rendering far shorter than the padding+font alone should have produced,
   clipping their own label text entirely (found live: "too thin vertically" and "tab header's
   texts are not seen" were the same bug -- a squashed box with its text clipped inside). 26dp (up
   from an initial 24dp alongside the 9dp->10dp font bump below) comfortably fits a 10dp font. */
.option-tab-row {
	display: flex;
	width: 100%;
	height: 26dp;
	margin-top: 2dp;
	margin-bottom: 8dp;
}
.option-tab-btn {
	flex: 1 1 0;
	margin: 0 1.5dp;
	height: 26dp;
	line-height: 26dp;
	text-align: center;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	font-size: 10dp;
	color: token(text-secondary);
	background-color: #13140f;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 255, 255, 10),
		transparent 45%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #26251d, #13140f 58%, #0a0b09);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #4a4738;
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(232, 215, 168, 14),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 #000000,
		0 1dp 2dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 153);
}
.option-tab-btn:hover {
	color: token(accent-gold-hover);
	border-color: #776646;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 255, 255, 13),
		transparent 45%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #302b1f, #18160f 58%, #0c0c09);
}
.option-tab-btn.active-tab {
	color: #171006;
	background-color: token(accent-gold);
	border-color: token(accent-gold-hover);
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 241, 199, 30),
		transparent 45%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-gold-hover), token(accent-gold) 58%, #6b5220);
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(255, 245, 214, 60),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 120),
		0 1dp 3dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 153);
}

/* Each tab's row group -- a flex column of its own, same as #content itself, so its own rows
   still stack top-to-bottom once spliced in. Needs the .hidden override below: this file loads
   after base.rcss, so its own display:flex would otherwise tie with (and silently beat) base.rcss's
   global .hidden{display:none} at equal specificity, leaving a "hidden" tab visibly rendered --
   the exact bug window_shell.rcss's own .window-shell-header-rail.hidden rule already fixed once
   for its header rail; same fix, same reason. */
.option-tab-panel {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	width: 100%;
}
.option-tab-panel.hidden {
	display: none;
}

/* Each settings row is a small flex line inside window_shell's own flex-column #content -- native
   used fixed per-row px offsets; this reproduces the same top-to-bottom order via normal flow
   instead (architecture-principles.md's own guidance: traceable intent, not literal numbers). */
.option-row {
	position: relative;
	width: 100%;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	flex-shrink: 0;
	margin-bottom: 6dp;
}
.option-checkbox-row {
	height: 18dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
/* Not base.rcss's own .checkbox-label -- that's position:absolute, glyph-first (built for
   login.rml's standalone checkboxes), which doesn't line up with a column of sibling rows. This
   row uses .option-row-label (below) instead, label-first in the markup, same as every other row
   type -- see option_window.rml's own comment. */
.checkbox-box {
	flex-shrink: 0;
}
/* Checkmark is a single 6x3 box with a left+bottom border rotated -45deg, same technique
   login.rcss's own modern checkbox already proves -- positioned against .checkbox-box itself
   (its containing block), unaffected by where that box sits within the row. */
.checkbox-tick {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	left: 2dp;
	top: 1dp;
	width: 6dp;
	height: 3dp;
	border-left: 2dp #171006;
	border-bottom: 2dp #171006;
	transform: rotate(-45deg);
}
.checkbox-box.checked .checkbox-tick {
	display: block;
}

/* 148dp -- widened from 130dp alongside the 9dp->10dp font bump (see #panel's own comment on why:
   reported live as smaller than this theme's own sibling windows). Puts the control column's own
   start near the row's horizontal center (well width 292dp: 320dp panel - 14dp*2 margins) instead
   of flush against the label. The two longest labels in this window were also shortened in
   Game.en.resx (see legacy's own copy of this comment) -- kept in sync across both themes even
   though modern's own wider well needed it less. New control width: 292-148=144dp. */
.option-row-label {
	width: 148dp;
	flex-shrink: 0;
	white-space: nowrap;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 10dp;
	color: token(text-secondary);
}

.option-slider-row,
.option-select-row {
	height: 20dp;
}

/* First real use of RmlUi's native <input type="range"> in this codebase -- drives the same
   WidgetSlider class as scrollbars, styled via the same slidertrack/sliderbar sub-elements
   generic_confirm_dialog.rcss already proves render/receive-input correctly in this build. Arrow
   sub-elements collapsed to 0 like that file's own scrollbar rule -- drag/click-only, matching
   native's own slider affordance (no stepper buttons).

   pointer-events: auto is required directly on this element, not just an ancestor -- same reason
   generic_confirm_dialog.rcss's own .gcd-text-col states it explicitly right next to its
   scrollbar rules: base.rcss's `body { pointer-events: none; }` inherits down to every descendant
   that doesn't opt back in, and WidgetSlider's generated slidertrack/sliderbar are real DOM
   children of THIS element (the <input> itself), inheriting whatever ITS OWN computed value is.
   .window-shell-content resetting to auto further up the tree covers ordinary rows fine, but
   leaving this un-stated here relies on that inheritance chain never getting interrupted anywhere
   in between -- explicit here removes the doubt (found live: the slider never moved on drag). */
.option-slider {
	flex-grow: 1;
	height: 12dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.option-slider slidertrack {
	height: 4dp;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 140);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
}
.option-slider sliderbar {
	width: 10dp;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-gold-hover), token(accent-gold));
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
}
.option-slider sliderarrowdec,
.option-slider sliderarrowinc {
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
}

/* First real use of RmlUi's native <select>/<option> in this codebase -- see option_window.rml's
   own comment on why (spike before trusting; a data-for custom dropdown is the fallback).
   pointer-events: auto explicit here too, same reasoning as .option-slider's own comment. */
.option-select {
	flex-grow: 1;
	height: 18dp;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 140);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-frame);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	color: token(text-secondary);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 10dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
}

/* .btn already defaults to position:absolute (base.rcss) -- kept, not overridden to static, since
   the close button is meant to sit at a FIXED spot regardless of which tab is active, not flow
   after whatever that tab's own last row happens to be (found live: the button visibly jumped up
   or down depending on the active tab's row count). option_window.rml parses this element inside
   #content (window_shell's only splice target) but OptionWindow.cpp moves it into
   window_shell.rml's own #window_shell_footer right after load (UI::RmlBridge::
   PromoteToWindowShellFooter(), matching generic_confirm_dialog.rcss's own direct-#panel-child
   buttons) -- bottom:0 here resolves against that footer's own box (window_shell.rcss's own
   .window-shell-footer, itself position:absolute against #panel), not #content's. left:50%+
   transform centers it horizontally the same way .center-both does. */
.option-close-btn {
	left: 50%;
	bottom: 0;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
}
